04-01
00:02:27.099 --> 00:02:31.600
Start with questions A one to A three on page one of the worksheet.

04-02
00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:40.951
First, define the four quantities: liquid composition, vapor
composition, overall composition, and activity coefficient.

04-03
00:02:41.131 --> 00:02:47.382
Be precise about which quantities describe a phase and which describe
the entire mixture.

04-04
00:02:47.562 --> 00:02:51.893
For A two, use the numerical observation printed on the slide.

04-05
00:02:52.073 --> 00:03:01.374
Multiply vapor composition by total pressure, then divide by liquid
composition times the pure-component saturation pressure.

04-06
00:03:01.554 --> 00:03:04.946
Use the full worksheet values before rounding.

04-07
00:03:05.126 --> 00:03:09.009
The pressures use the same units, so those units cancel.

04-08
00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:15.461
The stated assumptions are ideal vapor behavior and a negligible
Poynting correction.

04-09
00:03:15.641 --> 00:03:22.787
For A three, write a short explanation of whether a small fitting
error proves thermodynamic consistency.

04-10
00:03:22.967 --> 00:03:26.637
Pause the video now and attempt all three questions.

04-11
00:03:26.817 --> 00:03:30.849
Allow about nine minutes, then resume to check your work.

05-01
00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:35.448
Check your definitions first.

05-02
00:03:35.628 --> 00:03:40.449
Liquid composition, x one, is a mole fraction within the liquid.

05-03
00:03:40.629 --> 00:03:45.515
Vapor composition, y one, is a mole fraction within the vapor.

05-04
00:03:45.695 --> 00:03:51.135
Overall composition, z one, describes the combined feed or mixture.

05-05
00:03:51.315 --> 00:03:54.088
These do not specify phase amounts.

05-06
00:03:54.268 --> 00:04:00.647
With the pure-liquid standard state, activity is liquid mole fraction
multiplied by gamma.

05-07
00:04:00.827 --> 00:04:06.672
Gamma is dimensionless and measures the departure from the ideal-
mixture activity.

05-08
00:04:06.852 --> 00:04:12.697
Your calculation should give approximately one point four nine one
five four five.

05-09
00:04:12.877 --> 00:04:19.213
Its natural logarithm is approximately zero point three nine nine
eight one three.

05-10
00:04:19.393 --> 00:04:23.063
Gamma is not a composition or a phase fraction.

05-11
00:04:23.243 --> 00:04:28.896
For A three, fitting asks how well a chosen model reproduces the
observations.

05-12
00:04:29.076 --> 00:04:39.380
Consistency asks whether properties inferred from those observations
satisfy a thermodynamic constraint, using the appropriate assumptions.

05-13
00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:44.189
A small fitting error alone does not answer that second question.

06-01
00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:50.943
To understand the constraint, we need one energy function.

06-02
00:04:51.123 --> 00:05:01.150
Excess Gibbs energy measures the difference between the actual mixture
and an ideal mixture at the same temperature, pressure, and
composition.

06-03
00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:04.765
It is not the entire Gibbs energy of mixing.

06-04
00:05:04.945 --> 00:05:10.214
The ideal contribution will also matter when we examine stability
later.

06-05
00:05:10.394 --> 00:05:19.759
The partial molar excess Gibbs energy of a component is R T times the
natural logarithm of its activity coefficient.

06-06
00:05:19.939 --> 00:05:27.577
The mixture's molar excess Gibbs energy is the composition-weighted
sum of those partial molar quantities.

06-07
00:05:27.757 --> 00:05:34.669
Dividing by R T gives the dimensionless function shown here, which we
will call g tilde.

06-08
00:05:34.849 --> 00:05:39.947
For a binary mixture, take x as the mole fraction of component one.

06-09
00:05:40.127 --> 00:05:46.866
Then g tilde equals x times log gamma one, plus one minus x times log
gamma two.

06-10
00:05:47.046 --> 00:05:50.587
Every log here means the natural logarithm.

06-11
00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:52.452
The weights add to one.

06-12
00:05:52.632 --> 00:05:58.712
Both activity coefficients describe partial molar information from the
same energy function.

06-13
00:05:58.892 --> 00:06:02.860
They therefore cannot be selected as two unrelated curves.

06-14
00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:06.006
Use this definition to begin B one.

07-01
00:06:08.536 --> 00:06:16.536
At fixed temperature and pressure, the Gibbs-Duhem equation tells us
how the two activity coefficients must change together.

07-02
00:06:16.716 --> 00:06:20.150
Multiply the change in log gamma one by x.

07-03
00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:24.853
Multiply the change in log gamma two by one minus x.

07-04
00:06:25.033 --> 00:06:26.825
The sum must be zero.

07-05
00:06:27.005 --> 00:06:35.218
These are composition-weighted changes, not an instruction that the
activity coefficients themselves must add to one.

07-06
00:06:35.398 --> 00:06:45.510
At an interior composition, if one log activity coefficient increases
as composition changes, the other must change in a compatible way.

07-07
00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:49.594
The weights determine the relationship between those changes.

07-08
00:06:49.774 --> 00:06:53.316
An ideal mixture is a simple special case.

07-09
00:06:53.496 --> 00:07:00.002
Both gamma values equal one, both logarithms are zero, and the
condition is satisfied.

07-10
00:07:00.182 --> 00:07:04.406
But a nonideal mixture can also satisfy Gibbs-Duhem.

07-11
00:07:04.586 --> 00:07:10.214
Large gamma values do not, by themselves, indicate inconsistent data.

07-12
00:07:10.394 --> 00:07:15.109
What matters is the relationship between their composition
dependences.

08-01
00:07:17.639 --> 00:07:21.970
Now differentiate g tilde with respect to composition.

08-02
00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:27.099
Use the product rule: both the weights and the logarithms depend on x.

08-03
00:07:27.279 --> 00:07:36.367
Differentiating x times log gamma one produces log gamma one, plus x
times the derivative of log gamma one.

08-04
00:07:36.547 --> 00:07:47.726
Differentiating one minus x times log gamma two produces minus log
gamma two, plus one minus x times the derivative of log gamma two.

08-05
00:07:47.906 --> 00:07:50.700
Collect the two plain logarithms first.

08-06
00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:56.299
Their difference is the natural logarithm of gamma one divided by
gamma two.

08-07
00:07:56.479 --> 00:07:58.378
We call that quantity L.

08-08
00:07:58.558 --> 00:08:02.824
The remaining terms are the composition-weighted derivatives.

08-09
00:08:03.004 --> 00:08:07.506
At fixed temperature and pressure, Gibbs-Duhem makes their sum zero.

08-10
00:08:07.686 --> 00:08:13.467
The result is compact: the slope of g tilde equals L at the same
composition.

08-11
00:08:13.647 --> 00:08:16.911
This gives us two ways to describe the same slope.

08-12
00:08:17.091 --> 00:08:25.496
We can estimate it from neighboring excess-energy values, or calculate
L from the activity coefficients at the center.

08-13
00:08:25.676 --> 00:08:28.876
Their difference becomes our local residual.

08-14
00:08:29.056 --> 00:08:34.987
Write the product-rule steps in B one, rather than jumping straight to
the final equation.

09-01
00:08:37.517 --> 00:08:42.658
Before applying that result to measurements, ask how the measurements
were collected.

09-02
00:08:42.838 --> 00:08:45.812
Temperature, pressure, or both may change.

09-03
00:08:45.992 --> 00:08:52.008
Along a general path, the weighted changes in log gamma include two
additional terms.

09-04
00:08:52.188 --> 00:09:02.023
The temperature term is minus the molar excess enthalpy divided by R
times temperature squared, multiplied by the temperature change.

09-05
00:09:02.203 --> 00:09:09.328
The pressure term is molar excess volume divided by R T, multiplied by
the pressure change.

09-06
00:09:09.508 --> 00:09:13.626
When both temperature and pressure are fixed, both terms vanish.

09-07
00:09:13.806 --> 00:09:20.867
For isothermal vapor-liquid equilibrium, pressure usually varies
across the composition range.

09-08
00:09:21.047 --> 00:09:25.996
Lab zero three neglects the excess-volume pressure contribution.

09-09
00:09:26.176 --> 00:09:32.230
That is an assumption to state, not an exact consequence of holding
temperature fixed.

09-10
00:09:32.410 --> 00:09:35.460
For isobaric data, temperature varies.

09-11
00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:40.120
The excess-enthalpy correction is then needed for the local test.

09-12
00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:48.834
Real data without independent excess-enthalpy information cannot
receive this lab's local consistency verdict.

09-13
00:09:49.014 --> 00:09:55.879
A synthetic generator can supply its own correction, but that is not
independent calorimetry.

10-01
00:09:58.409 --> 00:10:02.100
The methods in this table examine different evidence.

10-02
00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:08.082
The isothermal area method tests an integral over the full composition
range.

10-03
00:10:08.262 --> 00:10:13.788
It needs suitable endpoint information and the stated pressure
approximation.

10-04
00:10:13.968 --> 00:10:18.533
Positive and negative departures can cancel in that integral.

10-05
00:10:18.713 --> 00:10:24.089
The local differential method examines the slope relationship across
composition.

10-06
00:10:24.269 --> 00:10:33.520
It can reveal a localized departure, but differentiation also makes it
sensitive to point spacing, rounding, and noise.

10-07
00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:36.494
Its path corrections must be appropriate.

10-08
00:10:36.674 --> 00:10:42.989
Herington uses an empirical area allowance for an isobaric boiling-
temperature range.

10-09
00:10:43.169 --> 00:10:46.348
It does not provide measured excess enthalpy.

10-10
00:10:46.528 --> 00:10:54.826
A Van Ness-type test instead examines how well a selected model
reproduces pressure and vapor composition.

10-11
00:10:55.006 --> 00:10:59.465
That result depends on the chosen model and the property assumptions.

10-12
00:10:59.645 --> 00:11:01.842
Use precise status words.

10-13
00:11:02.022 --> 00:11:06.502
Not applicable means the method does not match the situation.

10-14
00:11:06.682 --> 00:11:10.373
Not assessed can mean required information is missing.

10-15
00:11:10.553 --> 00:11:14.393
Not run means the calculation has not been performed.

10-16
00:11:14.573 --> 00:11:21.165
Outside this criterion means a calculated diagnostic exceeds its
stated limit.

10-17
00:11:21.345 --> 00:11:26.316
In B three, identify missing information before interpreting a number.

11-01
00:11:28.846 --> 00:11:32.728
Here is the local calculation we will actually use.

11-02
00:11:32.908 --> 00:11:39.735
First, calculate g tilde at every composition using the weighted log
activity coefficients.

11-03
00:11:39.915 --> 00:11:43.050
Second, estimate its slope from neighboring points.

11-04
00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:48.712
Third, subtract L, the center-point log gamma one minus log gamma two.

11-05
00:11:48.892 --> 00:11:56.484
That difference is the residual r, under fixed conditions or the
stated isothermal approximation.

11-06
00:11:56.664 --> 00:11:59.075
The residual is dimensionless.

11-07
00:11:59.255 --> 00:12:08.023
A positive value means the estimated slope exceeds the center log
ratio; a negative value means it is smaller.

11-08
00:12:08.203 --> 00:12:12.491
Its sign alone does not identify a physical cause.

11-09
00:12:12.671 --> 00:12:22.484
Lab zero three summarizes the absolute residuals over interior points
by taking their mean and multiplying by one hundred.

11-10
00:12:22.664 --> 00:12:26.312
The teaching threshold for that scaled score is five.

11-11
00:12:26.492 --> 00:12:31.250
It is not a probability or a percentage of incorrect measurements.

11-12
00:12:31.430 --> 00:12:35.355
One residual is also not the full-data average.

11-13
00:12:35.535 --> 00:12:44.346
Our lab uses three-point finite differences, rather than the NIST or
Kojima point test based on a Padé representation.

11-14
00:12:44.526 --> 00:12:45.678
Pause now.

11-15
00:12:45.858 --> 00:12:51.596
Complete B one to B three on worksheet page one using Lab guide page
one.

11-16
00:12:51.776 --> 00:13:00.475
Allow about fifteen minutes to finish the derivation, path table, and
missing-information explanation before continuing.

12-01
00:13:03.005 --> 00:13:07.165
We can now connect the local slope relationship to an area.

12-02
00:13:07.345 --> 00:13:15.238
Under the chosen pure-liquid standard states, the molar excess Gibbs
energy becomes zero at each pure endpoint.

12-03
00:13:15.418 --> 00:13:21.776
At one endpoint only component two remains; at the other only
component one remains.

12-04
00:13:21.956 --> 00:13:31.982
Integrating L from zero to one is the same as integrating the
derivative of g tilde, provided the slope relationship applies.

12-05
00:13:32.162 --> 00:13:37.901
The integral is therefore the endpoint difference in g tilde: zero
minus zero.

12-06
00:13:38.081 --> 00:13:40.406
That gives a zero signed area.

12-07
00:13:40.586 --> 00:13:48.574
This is a necessary global check under fixed temperature and pressure,
or under the stated isothermal approximation.

12-08
00:13:48.754 --> 00:13:52.295
It is not sufficient to certify every measurement.

12-09
00:13:52.475 --> 00:14:00.731
Positive and negative contributions can balance even when the
composition-dependent behavior contains a problem.

12-10
00:14:00.911 --> 00:14:06.138
For the exercise, the synthetic endpoint limits are supplied
analytically.

12-11
00:14:06.318 --> 00:14:12.035
In a real experiment, interior points alone would not establish the
missing limits.

13-01
00:14:14.565 --> 00:14:17.701
Complete C one to C three on page two.

13-02
00:14:17.881 --> 00:14:23.257
The table supplies five compositions, with equal spacing of one
quarter.

13-03
00:14:23.437 --> 00:14:30.252
For each adjacent pair, average the two L values and multiply by the
composition interval.

13-04
00:14:30.432 --> 00:14:35.488
Keep the sign of each trapezoid, including the intervals below zero.

13-05
00:14:35.668 --> 00:14:39.658
Write down all four contributions before adding them.

13-06
00:14:39.838 --> 00:14:45.320
Next, add zero point two to every L value and repeat the calculation.

13-07
00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:51.260
You are changing the entire curve vertically, not changing the
composition spacing.

13-08
00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:56.326
Finally, explain why a zero total does not certify the measurements.

13-09
00:14:56.506 --> 00:15:01.775
Refer both to cancellation and to the information required at the
endpoints.

13-10
00:15:01.955 --> 00:15:05.240
Here, the synthetic endpoint limits were supplied.

13-11
00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:09.858
Pause now and allow about eight minutes for C one to C three.

13-12
00:15:10.038 --> 00:15:16.438
Calculate both cases and write your explanation before advancing to
the answer slide.

14-01
00:15:18.968 --> 00:15:31.874
The four baseline trapezoids are zero point three seven five, zero
point one two five, minus zero point one two five, and minus zero
point three seven five.

14-02
00:15:32.054 --> 00:15:35.050
They cancel, so the signed sum is zero.

14-03
00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:42.376
After raising every L value by zero point two, each interval gains
zero point zero five.

14-04
00:15:42.556 --> 00:15:45.991
The new contributions sum to zero point two.

14-05
00:15:46.171 --> 00:15:54.150
This also follows from adding a constant height of zero point two
across a total composition width of one.

14-06
00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:59.812
Our worksheet calls the signed integral S; Lab zero three labels it I.

14-07
00:15:59.992 --> 00:16:04.430
The lab also reports a normalized area imbalance, D.

14-08
00:16:04.610 --> 00:16:15.336
It takes the absolute difference between the positive area and the
magnitude of the negative area, divides by their sum, and multiplies
by one hundred.

14-09
00:16:15.516 --> 00:16:20.231
The negative-area magnitude is entered as a positive quantity.

14-10
00:16:20.411 --> 00:16:25.190
The isothermal teaching criterion is D below five percent.

14-11
00:16:25.370 --> 00:16:29.636
If both areas vanish, that normalization is undefined.

15-01
00:16:32.166 --> 00:16:35.388
Follow C four and Lab guide page one.

15-02
00:16:35.568 --> 00:16:40.282
We will calculate the local residual from the energy values first.

15-03
00:16:40.462 --> 00:16:44.537
These are synthetic values at fixed temperature and pressure.

15-04
00:16:44.717 --> 00:16:55.859
At x equal to zero point four, multiply zero point four by zero point
seven two, then add zero point six times zero point three two.

15-05
00:16:56.039 --> 00:16:58.578
The result is zero point four eight.

15-06
00:16:58.758 --> 00:17:06.544
At the middle composition, both logarithms are zero point five, so
their weighted sum is zero point five.

15-07
00:17:06.724 --> 00:17:12.932
At x equal to zero point six, the weighted sum returns to zero point
four eight.

15-08
00:17:13.112 --> 00:17:17.998
Estimate the slope at the center using the two neighboring energy
values.

15-09
00:17:18.178 --> 00:17:21.954
Their difference is zero, so the centered slope is zero.

15-10
00:17:22.134 --> 00:17:29.042
The center log ratio is also zero, because the two log activity
coefficients there are equal.

15-11
00:17:29.222 --> 00:17:31.910
Subtracting gives a zero residual.

15-12
00:17:32.090 --> 00:17:40.310
The middle energy is still calculated and recorded, although this
equally spaced central slope uses the two neighbors.

15-13
00:17:40.490 --> 00:17:49.770
A zero result at this one center is evidence about this calculation,
not a certificate for an entire experimental data set.

16-01
00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:58.700
Now change only the final log gamma one value, at x equal to zero
point six.

16-02
00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:03.595
Increase it from zero point three two to zero point three six.

16-03
00:18:03.775 --> 00:18:06.890
Leave the other five logarithms unchanged.

16-04
00:18:07.070 --> 00:18:09.651
Recalculate the affected energy.

16-05
00:18:09.831 --> 00:18:20.199
Zero point six times zero point three six, plus zero point four times
zero point seven two, gives zero point five zero four.

16-06
00:18:20.379 --> 00:18:23.984
The left-hand energy remains zero point four eight.

16-07
00:18:24.164 --> 00:18:27.300
Their difference is zero point zero two four.

16-08
00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:34.136
Divide by the composition span of zero point two, giving a slope of
zero point one two.

16-09
00:18:34.316 --> 00:18:40.716
The center log ratio is still zero, so the residual is positive zero
point one two.

16-10
00:18:40.896 --> 00:18:48.875
A neighboring measurement has changed the estimated center slope, even
though the center logarithms have not changed.

16-11
00:18:49.055 --> 00:18:50.975
Record this in C four.

16-12
00:18:51.155 --> 00:19:00.734
With real measurements, a nonzero residual could reflect measurement
error, numerical sensitivity, or an omitted path correction.

16-13
00:19:00.914 --> 00:19:08.444
The residual identifies a departure to investigate; it does not
identify its cause by itself.

17-01
00:19:10.974 --> 00:19:16.372
Now work independently on C five and C six, still on worksheet page
two.

17-02
00:19:16.552 --> 00:19:18.834
Use the new three-row table.

17-03
00:19:19.014 --> 00:19:23.281
First calculate g tilde at each of the three compositions.

17-04
00:19:23.461 --> 00:19:31.354
Then estimate the centered slope at x equal to zero point four using
the left and right energy values.

17-05
00:19:31.534 --> 00:19:34.372
Subtract the log ratio at the center.

17-06
00:19:34.552 --> 00:19:44.621
For the second calculation, change only the final log gamma two value
from zero point three seven five to zero point four two five.

17-07
00:19:44.801 --> 00:19:49.900
Recalculate the affected energy, the center slope, and the residual.

17-08
00:19:50.080 --> 00:19:54.453
Do not change the center log ratio or any other entry.

17-09
00:19:54.633 --> 00:20:06.159
For C six, state why a measured nonzero residual requires further
investigation, and why one center-point result cannot stand for the
full data set.

17-10
00:20:06.339 --> 00:20:11.203
Pause the video now and allow about nine minutes for these two
questions.

17-11
00:20:11.383 --> 00:20:15.010
Finish both calculations before opening the check.

18-01
00:20:17.540 --> 00:20:19.801
Check the energy values first.

18-02
00:20:19.981 --> 00:20:28.664
The clean table gives zero point three one five, zero point three six
zero, and zero point three seven five.

18-03
00:20:28.844 --> 00:20:35.601
The centered slope is the right energy minus the left energy, divided
by zero point two.

18-04
00:20:35.781 --> 00:20:37.935
That gives zero point three.

18-05
00:20:38.115 --> 00:20:45.625
The center log ratio is zero point five four minus zero point two
four, also zero point three.

18-06
00:20:45.805 --> 00:20:48.194
The clean residual is zero.

18-07
00:20:48.374 --> 00:20:54.859
After changing the last log gamma two, the right-hand energy becomes
zero point four.

18-08
00:20:55.039 --> 00:20:59.007
The centered slope is then zero point four two five.

18-09
00:20:59.187 --> 00:21:08.851
Subtract the unchanged center log ratio of zero point three, leaving a
residual of positive zero point one two five.

18-10
00:21:09.031 --> 00:21:13.298
Finite differences depend on spacing, rounding, and noise.

18-11
00:21:13.478 --> 00:21:21.627
Our hand calculation assumes equal spacing; the lab's three-point
formula also handles unequal spacing.

18-12
00:21:21.807 --> 00:21:29.231
Neither a single residual nor a small averaged score establishes a
universal certificate.

18-13
00:21:29.411 --> 00:21:35.043
Keep your corrections, then open Lab guide pages two and three for the
demonstration.

19-01
00:21:37.573 --> 00:21:41.413
We are ready to connect the hand calculations to Lab Three.

19-02
00:21:41.593 --> 00:21:47.097
Keep worksheet page three and Lab guide pages two and three beside the
browser.

19-03
00:21:47.277 --> 00:21:49.859
First we establish a clean reference.

19-04
00:21:50.039 --> 00:21:54.945
Then we introduce two controlled errors, changing one setting at a
time.

19-05
00:21:55.125 --> 00:22:01.547
We compare forty one and one hundred sixty one observations for the
localized error.

19-06
00:22:01.727 --> 00:22:08.404
Finally, we return to the clean reference, run the model reproduction
test, and save the evidence.

19-07
00:22:08.584 --> 00:22:12.381
The following views were captured from the working Lab interface.

19-08
00:22:12.561 --> 00:22:18.215
They show the actual controls and results, rather than a drawing of
the application.

19-09
00:22:18.395 --> 00:22:24.091
Before each altered case, write a prediction in the appropriate row of
your worksheet.

19-10
00:22:24.271 --> 00:22:27.684
If you need more time to predict, pause manually.

19-11
00:22:27.864 --> 00:22:33.133
The data are synthetic, so we know which defect was deliberately
introduced.

19-12
00:22:33.313 --> 00:22:39.137
That gives us a controlled way to learn what each diagnostic can and
cannot detect.

20-01
00:22:41.667 --> 00:22:45.550
Open Lab Three from the link on the lesson page or slide.

20-02
00:22:45.730 --> 00:22:49.506
Scroll to the workbench headed Which error can you detect.

20-03
00:22:49.686 --> 00:22:55.118
In Data set, select Synthetic, isothermal, three hundred fifty kelvin.

20-04
00:22:55.298 --> 00:22:59.138
In Controlled perturbation, select Consistent reference.

20-05
00:22:59.318 --> 00:23:02.411
Set Sampling resolution to forty one points.

20-06
00:23:02.591 --> 00:23:10.058
Do not leave it at the default eighty one points, because we need the
same baseline for every comparison.

20-07
00:23:10.238 --> 00:23:16.915
The amplitude control reads zero point two five but is disabled for
the consistent reference.

20-08
00:23:17.095 --> 00:23:19.762
No perturbation is applied in that case.

20-09
00:23:19.942 --> 00:23:29.179
The results update when the input changes; you do not need to press
the modeling button to calculate the area and differential
diagnostics.

20-10
00:23:29.359 --> 00:23:32.559
Check the observation count above the scorecards.

20-11
00:23:32.739 --> 00:23:38.755
It should say forty one observations, forty one activity pairs, and
zero excluded.

20-12
00:23:38.935 --> 00:23:41.879
The synthetic endpoint limits are supplied.

20-13
00:23:42.059 --> 00:23:46.283
Record those settings under D one before recording any score.

20-14
00:23:46.463 --> 00:23:56.277
Also record that this is an isothermal path using the teaching
approximation that neglects the excess-volume pressure contribution.

21-01
00:23:58.807 --> 00:24:01.175
Read the area scorecard first.

21-02
00:24:01.355 --> 00:24:08.267
With these settings, the displayed area percentage is approximately
zero point zero seven nine.

21-03
00:24:08.447 --> 00:24:12.841
The differential score is approximately zero point one one eight.

21-04
00:24:13.021 --> 00:24:15.880
Both meet their stated teaching criteria.

21-05
00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:21.607
Herington says Not applicable because we selected an isothermal
dataset.

21-06
00:24:21.787 --> 00:24:27.696
The modeling card says Not run, which simply records that we have not
fitted a model.

21-07
00:24:27.876 --> 00:24:33.359
Now use Inspect to select Log-activity ratio and integrated area.

21-08
00:24:33.539 --> 00:24:39.533
The curve crosses zero, with positive and negative contributions that
almost balance.

21-09
00:24:39.713 --> 00:24:50.060
The displayed positive and negative area magnitudes are about zero
point three nine three two nine and zero point three nine two six
seven.

21-10
00:24:50.240 --> 00:24:55.253
Their small difference gives the reported nonzero numerical integral.

21-11
00:24:55.433 --> 00:24:59.443
Next, change Inspect to Local Gibbs-Duhem residual.

21-12
00:24:59.623 --> 00:25:08.348
The clean reference has small residuals, most visible toward low
composition, rather than a perfectly flat line at zero.

21-13
00:25:08.528 --> 00:25:12.816
That is expected from finite differences on a finite grid.

21-14
00:25:12.996 --> 00:25:17.988
Keep the vertical-axis scale in view: these clean residuals are small.

21-15
00:25:18.168 --> 00:25:23.245
When you compare the next case, read both the shape and its numerical
scale.

21-16
00:25:23.425 --> 00:25:30.145
Automatic plot scaling can otherwise make two very different
magnitudes look deceptively similar.

22-01
00:25:32.675 --> 00:25:38.371
Before the next result appears, write two short predictions in D two
and D three.

22-02
00:25:38.551 --> 00:25:48.791
First, imagine an error that is positive in one composition region and
negative in another, with exactly zero integral over the full range.

22-03
00:25:48.971 --> 00:25:52.278
Would an area test necessarily detect it?

22-04
00:25:52.458 --> 00:25:55.701
What would you expect from a local residual plot?

22-05
00:25:55.881 --> 00:26:03.475
Second, imagine an error concentrated in a narrow region near
composition zero point three five.

22-06
00:26:03.655 --> 00:26:10.269
Could an average score remain below a threshold even when the local
graph shows a clear defect?

22-07
00:26:10.449 --> 00:26:13.969
Do not try to guess the exact numerical scores.

22-08
00:26:14.149 --> 00:26:18.415
Predict the type of evidence that would change, and explain why.

22-09
00:26:18.595 --> 00:26:24.097
You can revise the prediction later, but keep the original beside the
correction.

22-10
00:26:24.277 --> 00:26:28.778
Pause the video manually now if you have not written both predictions.

22-11
00:26:28.958 --> 00:26:38.722
We will keep temperature, amplitude, and the number of points fixed so
that the perturbation type is the only change in the first comparison.

23-01
00:26:41.252 --> 00:26:45.869
In Controlled perturbation, choose Canceling errors, zero net area.

23-02
00:26:46.049 --> 00:26:51.403
Keep amplitude at zero point two five and sampling at forty one
points.

23-03
00:26:51.583 --> 00:26:57.279
The area percentage remains small, approximately zero point zero seven
four.

23-04
00:26:57.459 --> 00:26:59.977
It still meets the area criterion.

23-05
00:27:00.157 --> 00:27:08.071
The differential score now rises to approximately fifteen point five
nine nine, outside its teaching criterion.

23-06
00:27:08.251 --> 00:27:12.070
Select Local Gibbs-Duhem residual under Inspect.

23-07
00:27:12.250 --> 00:27:19.333
The residual has a broad negative region, a positive central region,
and another negative region.

23-08
00:27:19.513 --> 00:27:23.779
Its magnitude is much larger than in the clean reference.

23-09
00:27:23.959 --> 00:27:26.029
Why do the tests disagree?

23-10
00:27:26.209 --> 00:27:31.329
The generator adds a cosine-shaped error to the log-activity ratio.

23-11
00:27:31.509 --> 00:27:36.927
Its signed integral is exactly zero over the full composition
interval.

23-12
00:27:37.107 --> 00:27:45.150
At the same time, the weighted changes in the two log activity
coefficients cancel in excess Gibbs energy.

23-13
00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:50.429
The energy curve therefore stays unchanged while the log ratio
changes.

23-14
00:27:50.609 --> 00:27:57.521
The derivative identity is broken locally even though the extra signed
area cancels globally.

23-15
00:27:57.701 --> 00:28:05.189
A small numerical area is consequently compatible with deliberately
inconsistent local behavior.

23-16
00:28:05.369 --> 00:28:15.929
In your worksheet, distinguish the mathematical cancellation of the
imposed error from the small integration error still present in the
numerical baseline.

23-17
00:28:16.109 --> 00:28:23.127
The evidence supports a specific statement: the area criterion alone
missed this constructed defect.

24-01
00:28:25.657 --> 00:28:31.268
Now select Localized defect near x one equals zero point three five.

24-02
00:28:31.448 --> 00:28:36.824
Keep the amplitude at zero point two five and begin with forty one
points.

24-03
00:28:37.004 --> 00:28:46.502
The area percentage is approximately two point zero one three, and the
differential score is approximately one point six seven two.

24-04
00:28:46.682 --> 00:28:48.389
Both are below five.

24-05
00:28:48.569 --> 00:28:55.828
However, the local residual graph contains a distinct negative trough
near zero point three five.

24-06
00:28:56.008 --> 00:29:02.984
A criterion based on an average can dilute an error that occupies only
a narrow region.

24-07
00:29:03.164 --> 00:29:08.561
That is why you must inspect the plotted residual as well as the
scorecard.

24-08
00:29:08.741 --> 00:29:15.760
Record the forty one point results before changing Sampling resolution
to one hundred sixty one points.

24-09
00:29:15.940 --> 00:29:25.796
The area percentage becomes approximately one point nine four two,
while the differential score becomes approximately one point five six
six.

24-10
00:29:25.976 --> 00:29:31.102
The trough remains, and the denser grid describes its shape with more
points.

24-11
00:29:31.282 --> 00:29:34.695
Neither result changes to an automatic failure.

24-12
00:29:34.875 --> 00:29:41.318
More points do not guarantee that an aggregate threshold will flag a
localized defect.

24-13
00:29:41.498 --> 00:29:48.282
This is also not a new noisy experiment: the teaching generator is
being sampled more densely.

24-14
00:29:48.462 --> 00:29:55.075
A screenshot with the trough marked near zero point three five is
useful evidence for E one.

24-15
00:29:55.255 --> 00:30:05.047
Avoid claiming that this finite-difference exercise validates a
laboratory dataset or duplicates the NIST point-test implementation.

25-01
00:30:07.577 --> 00:30:15.812
For D five, return Controlled perturbation to Consistent reference and
Sampling resolution to forty one points.

25-02
00:30:15.992 --> 00:30:19.939
Verify those settings before pressing Run modeling test.

25-03
00:30:20.119 --> 00:30:27.692
The application reports convergence using thirty nine interior points,
with no fitted parameter at a bound.

25-04
00:30:27.872 --> 00:30:31.733
The two pure endpoints are excluded from this fitting step.

25-05
00:30:31.913 --> 00:30:38.868
Pressure and vapor-composition errors both round to zero point zero
zero zero on the scorecard.

25-06
00:30:39.048 --> 00:30:45.625
They are very small numerical errors, not a promise of exact
experimental accuracy.

25-07
00:30:45.805 --> 00:30:54.978
This synthetic reference is generated from the same family of
consistent activity models, so a very close reproduction is expected.

25-08
00:30:55.158 --> 00:31:02.241
Under Inspect, choose Modeling residuals to see both pressure and
vapor-composition residuals.

25-09
00:31:02.421 --> 00:31:13.727
The method fits two constant NRTL interaction parameters for this
isothermal case, with the nonrandomness parameter fixed at zero point
four seven.

25-10
00:31:13.907 --> 00:31:21.886
It asks whether the selected model reproduces the observations under
the stated vapor-property approximation.

25-11
00:31:22.066 --> 00:31:28.466
It does not independently prove that observations obey every
thermodynamic constraint.

25-12
00:31:28.646 --> 00:31:33.105
Click Export all results to download the current results and settings.

25-13
00:31:33.285 --> 00:31:41.327
Save the file beside your worksheet, and retain screenshots of the
