26-14
00:33:54.979 --> 00:33:57.923
Use the Lab guide if you lose your place.

26-15
00:33:58.103 --> 00:34:00.961
The player will pause before the check slide.

26-16
00:34:01.141 --> 00:34:06.517
Continue when your evidence is recorded, not merely when a timer
expires.

27-01
00:34:09.047 --> 00:34:13.314
Compare your completed investigation with these conclusions.

27-02
00:34:13.494 --> 00:34:17.547
The consistent reference has small numerical residuals.

27-03
00:34:17.727 --> 00:34:28.373
The canceling-error case satisfies the area criterion while showing a
much larger local residual and an outside-criterion differential
score.

27-04
00:34:28.553 --> 00:34:39.475
The localized defect is a different lesson: both aggregate scores meet
their teaching thresholds, while the local graph still reveals a
narrow imposed error.

27-05
00:34:39.655 --> 00:34:51.517
Increasing the resolution changes the numerical scores and resolves
the trough more densely; it does not erase the defect or guarantee a
different threshold decision.

27-06
00:34:51.697 --> 00:35:00.870
Finally, the clean-reference NRTL fit reproduces pressure and vapor
composition very closely under its assumptions.

27-07
00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:04.591
That result answers a model-reproduction question.

27-08
00:35:04.771 --> 00:35:12.281
It does not override the need to examine the data path, local
evidence, or missing correction properties.

27-09
00:35:12.461 --> 00:35:28.269
A useful E two conclusion might state that an area result alone cannot
rule out canceling errors, cite your area and local scores, and
identify the synthetic setting and finite-difference approximation as
limitations.

27-10
00:35:28.449 --> 00:35:31.841
Keep any correction beside the original attempt.

27-11
00:35:32.021 --> 00:35:39.701
We now move from checking property relationships to deciding which
phase arrangement minimizes Gibbs energy.

28-01
00:35:42.231 --> 00:35:44.321
Take a ten-minute break now.

28-02
00:35:44.501 --> 00:35:48.981
Save your worksheet and Lab Three evidence before leaving the screen.

28-03
00:35:49.161 --> 00:35:54.921
This break is part of the approximately three-hour lesson, not an
additional assignment.

28-04
00:35:55.101 --> 00:35:58.365
When you return, have worksheet page four ready.

28-05
00:35:58.545 --> 00:36:06.567
We will begin with the distinction between a locally stable
homogeneous liquid and the globally stable phase arrangement.

28-06
00:36:06.747 --> 00:36:15.365
You will calculate phase amounts by hand, then use Lab Four to compare
three overall compositions at the same model setting.

28-07
00:36:15.545 --> 00:36:19.492
The player will remain paused until you choose to continue.

29-01
00:36:22.022 --> 00:36:24.027
We now change the question.

29-02
00:36:24.207 --> 00:36:31.909
Consistency asks whether the composition dependence of the properties
obeys a thermodynamic relationship.

29-03
00:36:32.089 --> 00:36:38.446
Stability asks which admissible arrangement of phases has the lowest
total Gibbs energy.

29-04
00:36:38.626 --> 00:36:48.034
At fixed temperature and pressure, compare one homogeneous liquid with
possible splits that preserve the total amount of each component.

29-05
00:36:48.214 --> 00:36:59.457
The mixing Gibbs energy contains the ideal mixing terms, x log x and
one minus x times log one minus x, plus the excess Gibbs energy.

29-06
00:36:59.637 --> 00:37:06.186
A sufficiently unfavorable excess contribution can make the total
curve nonconvex.

29-07
00:37:06.366 --> 00:37:11.742
The homogeneous liquid can then sit above a lower-energy two-liquid
mixture.

29-08
00:37:11.922 --> 00:37:17.575
Nothing in that statement requires the activity model to violate
Gibbs-Duhem.

29-09
00:37:17.755 --> 00:37:26.353
Indeed, a model derived consistently from one excess Gibbs-energy
function can predict liquid-liquid separation.

29-10
00:37:26.533 --> 00:37:35.151
For F one, explain why checking a property identity does not tell us
whether one liquid is the stable phase arrangement.

30-01
00:37:37.681 --> 00:37:44.871
Local curvature describes how the homogeneous liquid responds to a
small composition fluctuation.

30-02
00:37:45.051 --> 00:37:52.197
Positive curvature means that sufficiently small fluctuations increase
its bulk Gibbs energy.

30-03
00:37:52.377 --> 00:37:57.540
Negative curvature means that small fluctuations can lower the energy.

30-04
00:37:57.720 --> 00:38:04.065
However, a small-fluctuation test does not compare every possible
finite separation.

30-05
00:38:04.245 --> 00:38:09.322
That distinction separates stable and metastable homogeneous liquids.

30-06
00:38:09.502 --> 00:38:13.513
A stable liquid lies on the lower convex envelope.

30-07
00:38:13.693 --> 00:38:21.266
A metastable liquid has positive curvature but lies above a lower-
energy combination of two liquids.

30-08
00:38:21.446 --> 00:38:28.145
An unstable liquid has negative curvature and also fails the global
minimum requirement.

30-09
00:38:28.325 --> 00:38:36.943
For the one-parameter Margules model, the dimensionless excess Gibbs
energy is A times x times one minus x.

30-10
00:38:37.123 --> 00:38:44.697
The curvature of the total mixing function is one divided by x times
one minus x, minus two A.

30-11
00:38:44.877 --> 00:38:47.266
We will use A equal to three.

30-12
00:38:47.446 --> 00:38:57.259
This bulk equilibrium model does not calculate nucleation barriers,
interfaces, or how long a metastable liquid will persist.

31-01
00:38:59.789 --> 00:39:04.867
Imagine joining two points on the Gibbs-energy curve with a straight
line.

31-02
00:39:05.047 --> 00:39:15.948
The energy of a mixture of those two liquid compositions lies on that
line, because the total molar energy is the phase-fraction-weighted
average.

31-03
00:39:16.128 --> 00:39:22.400
At a candidate equilibrium pair, the line has the same slope as the
curve at both ends.

31-04
00:39:22.580 --> 00:39:28.980
That common-tangent condition corresponds to equality of the component
chemical potentials.

31-05
00:39:29.160 --> 00:39:33.811
But finding a stationary pair is only part of the calculation.

31-06
00:39:33.991 --> 00:39:40.305
The tangent must support the whole admitted curve: nowhere may another
composition lie below it.

31-07
00:39:40.485 --> 00:39:50.000
If the candidate line crosses above the curve, another admissible
state can lower the energy and the candidate is not globally stable.

31-08
00:39:50.180 --> 00:39:53.636
Also check that the two compositions are distinct.

31-09
00:39:53.816 --> 00:39:59.533
A solver returning the same composition twice has not established two
liquids.

31-10
00:39:59.713 --> 00:40:09.478
Complete F three by separating these checks: distinct phases,
chemical-potential equality, global support, and compatible amounts.

31-11
00:40:09.658 --> 00:40:14.672
Their material balance cannot substitute for the global energy
comparison.

32-01
00:40:17.202 --> 00:40:20.082
This is the hand-calculation work stop.

32-02
00:40:20.262 --> 00:40:25.339
Complete F two on worksheet page four before seeing the next slide.

32-03
00:40:25.519 --> 00:40:31.087
The supplied endpoints are zero point two zero and zero point eight
zero.

32-04
00:40:31.267 --> 00:40:35.875
Treat their common tangent as globally supported for this exercise.

32-05
00:40:36.055 --> 00:40:40.919
First use an overall composition of zero point five zero.

32-06
00:40:41.099 --> 00:40:44.000
Then repeat for zero point three five.

32-07
00:40:44.180 --> 00:40:55.856
The fraction of the component-one-rich liquid, beta, equals the
overall composition minus the alpha endpoint, divided by the distance
between the two endpoints.

32-08
00:40:56.036 --> 00:41:00.025
Here beta is a liquid label, not a vapor fraction.

32-09
00:41:00.205 --> 00:41:04.877
These illustrative endpoints belong only to the hand exercise.

32-10
00:41:05.057 --> 00:41:10.177
They are not the endpoints that Lab Four must produce for A equal to
three.

32-11
00:41:10.357 --> 00:41:16.053
Pause for about eight minutes, complete both balances, and then
continue to the check.

33-01
00:41:18.583 --> 00:41:25.452
For the feed at zero point five zero, the overall composition is
halfway between the two endpoints.

33-02
00:41:25.632 --> 00:41:29.728
The two liquid fractions are therefore equal: one half each.

33-03
00:41:29.908 --> 00:41:40.127
For the feed at zero point three five, the beta fraction is zero point
one five divided by zero point six zero, or one quarter.

33-04
00:41:40.307 --> 00:41:42.867
The alpha fraction is three quarters.

33-05
00:41:43.047 --> 00:41:54.567
Substituting these amounts gives three quarters times zero point two
zero plus one quarter times zero point eight zero, which reconstructs
zero point three five.

33-06
00:41:54.747 --> 00:41:56.902
The endpoints have not changed.

33-07
00:41:57.082 --> 00:42:08.900
Moving the overall composition inside the same tie line changes how
much of each liquid is present, rather than changing the compositions
of the coexisting liquids.

33-08
00:42:09.080 --> 00:42:17.678
If you insert a feed outside the endpoint interval, the expression
produces a negative fraction or a fraction above one.

33-09
00:42:17.858 --> 00:42:22.935
That is a warning that this two-phase construction does not represent
the feed.

34-01
00:42:25.465 --> 00:42:32.078
Before opening a named Lab Four preset, write your G one predictions
on worksheet page four.

34-02
00:42:32.258 --> 00:42:36.696
The model is one-parameter Margules, with A fixed at three.

34-03
00:42:36.876 --> 00:42:44.854
Compare overall compositions zero point zero two, zero point one two,
and zero point five zero.

34-04
00:42:45.034 --> 00:42:51.562
For each, predict the homogeneous-feed classification and the number
of liquids at equilibrium.

34-05
00:42:51.742 --> 00:42:53.918
These are two separate columns.

34-06
00:42:54.098 --> 00:43:00.626
You can calculate the curvature as one divided by z times one minus z,
minus six.

34-07
00:43:00.806 --> 00:43:04.945
A negative value gives a useful local diagnosis.

34-08
00:43:05.125 --> 00:43:09.413
A positive value leaves a global question unresolved.

34-09
00:43:09.593 --> 00:43:15.079
It does not, by itself, distinguish a stable feed from a metastable
one.

34-10
00:43:15.259 --> 00:43:19.142
Write your reasoning before the interface supplies the labels.

34-11
00:43:19.322 --> 00:43:28.200
The presets are named Stable, Metastable, and Unstable, so opening
them before predicting would remove the purpose of this step.

35-01
00:43:30.730 --> 00:43:36.234
Open Lab Four using the slide link, and keep Lab guide page four
available.

35-02
00:43:36.414 --> 00:43:43.600
Choose Margules, one parameter, as the liquid model, and set the
interaction parameter A to three.

35-03
00:43:43.780 --> 00:43:48.196
Use the feed mole-fraction field to enter the first composition.

35-04
00:43:48.376 --> 00:43:51.875
After typing a value, press Find equilibrium.

35-05
00:43:52.055 --> 00:43:56.926
Sliders update immediately, but a typed value requires that button.

35-06
00:43:57.106 --> 00:44:00.925
Keep the Gibbs energy and common tangent view visible.

35-07
00:44:01.105 --> 00:44:06.225
Read the homogeneous-feed label separately from the equilibrium phase
count.

35-08
00:44:06.405 --> 00:44:11.077
Open Verify the equilibrium to inspect the numerical evidence.

35-09
00:44:11.257 --> 00:44:19.882
We need the local curvature, the Gibbs-energy decrease, the balance,
and the coexistence checks for cases with two liquids.

35-10
00:44:20.062 --> 00:44:24.520
Repeat the calculation for the other feeds without changing A.

35-11
00:44:24.700 --> 00:44:29.308
This is a hypothetical fixed-temperature-and-pressure model exercise.

35-12
00:44:29.488 --> 00:44:33.862
No physical temperature is assigned to these parameter values.

36-01
00:44:36.392 --> 00:44:40.530
Now complete your independent Lab Four investigation.

36-02
00:44:40.710 --> 00:44:43.185
Allow about twenty-eight minutes here.

36-03
00:44:43.365 --> 00:44:53.605
In G two, run all three feeds at A equal to three and record the
homogeneous-feed classification and equilibrium phase count
separately.

36-04
00:44:53.785 --> 00:44:59.929
For the two-liquid cases, record both actual coexistence endpoints and
both fractions.

36-05
00:45:00.109 --> 00:45:11.032
Under G three, use your calculator to recompute the beta fraction from
the lab endpoints, then reconstruct the overall composition from the
weighted balance.

36-06
00:45:11.212 --> 00:45:16.545
Do not reuse zero point two and zero point eight from the hand
example.

36-07
00:45:16.725 --> 00:45:26.069
In G four, calculate the homogeneous and split Gibbs energies at zero
point one two, then compare their difference with the Lab.

36-08
00:45:26.249 --> 00:45:36.596
Inspect the entire common-tangent plot and the numerical checks,
rather than treating a small balance residual as complete proof of
equilibrium.

36-09
00:45:36.776 --> 00:45:41.981
For a one-liquid result, record the present liquid at the feed
composition.

36-10
00:45:42.161 --> 00:45:46.214
Do not invent a second liquid just to fill the table.

36-11
00:45:46.394 --> 00:45:52.432
Save each case with Export study JSON or a clearly labeled screenshot.

36-12
00:45:52.612 --> 00:45:57.433
Continue after the records and independent balances are complete.

37-01
00:45:59.963 --> 00:46:13.424
At A equal to three, the coexistence endpoints are approximately zero
point zero seven zero seven two zero one eight and zero point nine two
nine two seven nine eight two.

37-02
00:46:13.604 --> 00:46:18.383
The feed at zero point zero two lies outside that interval.

37-03
00:46:18.563 --> 00:46:23.064
It is globally stable as one liquid at the feed composition.

37-04
00:46:23.244 --> 00:46:27.895
Its positive curvature is about forty-five point zero two.

37-05
00:46:28.075 --> 00:46:38.178
At zero point one two, the curvature is also positive, about three
point four seven, but the homogeneous feed is metastable.

37-06
00:46:38.358 --> 00:46:44.950
It lies inside the miscibility gap, where two liquids provide a lower
total Gibbs energy.

37-07
00:46:45.130 --> 00:46:52.468
At zero point five zero, the curvature is negative two and the
homogeneous feed is unstable.

37-08
00:46:52.648 --> 00:46:58.643
Its stable equilibrium is also two liquids at the same coexistence
endpoints.

37-09
00:46:58.823 --> 00:47:07.335
The second and third cases therefore have different homogeneous-feed
labels but the same equilibrium phase compositions.

37-10
00:47:07.515 --> 00:47:15.408
This comparison is the central lesson: positive curvature does not
rule out a finite lower-energy split.

38-01
00:47:17.938 --> 00:47:23.229
For the feed at zero point one two, use the actual endpoint interval.

38-02
00:47:23.409 --> 00:47:38.812
Subtract about zero point zero seven zero seven two from zero point
one two, then divide by the difference between about zero point nine
two nine two eight and zero point zero seven zero seven two.

38-03
00:47:38.992 --> 00:47:45.285
The beta fraction is approximately zero point zero five seven three
nine eight two five.

38-04
00:47:45.465 --> 00:47:52.057
The alpha fraction is approximately zero point nine four two six zero
one seven five.

38-05
00:47:52.237 --> 00:47:56.610
Their weighted composition reconstructs zero point one two.

38-06
00:47:56.790 --> 00:48:01.420
For the symmetric feed at one half, the two fractions are one half.

38-07
00:48:01.600 --> 00:48:04.949
Now compare energy at zero point one two.

38-08
00:48:05.129 --> 00:48:13.620
The homogeneous mixing energy divided by R T is about minus zero point
zero five zero one two five.

38-09
00:48:13.800 --> 00:48:18.749
The split value is minus zero point zero five eight three four one.

38-10
00:48:18.929 --> 00:48:25.858
Their difference is the positive decrease, approximately zero point
zero zero eight two one six.

38-11
00:48:26.038 --> 00:48:31.030
The unstable feed has a larger decrease; the stable feed has none.

38-12
00:48:31.210 --> 00:48:37.994
These dimensionless differences explain the thermodynamic advantage,
not the separation speed.

38-13
00:48:38.174 --> 00:48:46.366
A valid balance checks the amounts; the globally supporting tangent
establishes the selected equilibrium compositions.

39-01
00:48:48.896 --> 00:48:56.299
The next four questions use new values, so solve them rather than
copying a result from the demonstration.

39-02
00:48:56.479 --> 00:48:59.487
Open worksheet page six, section H.

39-03
00:48:59.667 --> 00:49:10.718
On this slide, H one gives dimensionless excess Gibbs energies at
compositions zero point two, zero point three, and zero point four.

39-04
00:49:10.898 --> 00:49:15.634
Estimate the derivative at the center using the two neighboring
values.

39-05
00:49:15.814 --> 00:49:21.424
Then subtract the supplied center log-activity ratio, zero point seven
five.

39-06
00:49:21.604 --> 00:49:27.919
Explain whether the result is one local residual or the Lab's average
over many points.

39-07
00:49:28.099 --> 00:49:35.651
H two concerns real isobaric VLE data without independent excess-
enthalpy information.

39-08
00:49:35.831 --> 00:49:38.732
The raw local score is eight point four.

39-09
00:49:38.912 --> 00:49:46.656
Decide whether the information supports a failed consistency verdict,
and explain the role of the experimental path.

39-10
00:49:46.836 --> 00:49:52.362
Read H three and H four on the next slide before beginning the main
pause.

39-11
00:49:52.542 --> 00:49:54.120
Show your assumptions.

40-01
00:49:56.650 --> 00:50:07.210
H three gives candidate endpoints zero point one five and zero point
seven five, with an overall composition of zero point three nine.

40-02
00:50:07.390 --> 00:50:11.593
Calculate both phase fractions and reconstruct the feed.

40-03
00:50:11.773 --> 00:50:17.576
Then state what evidence is still needed to call the candidate a
stable equilibrium.

40-04
00:50:17.756 --> 00:50:24.838
The question does not supply a complete Gibbs curve, so do not claim
to have checked global support numerically.

40-05
00:50:25.018 --> 00:50:29.840
H four gives a hypothetical full-range isothermal dataset.

40-06
00:50:30.020 --> 00:50:45.288
Its area percentage is one point two, its local mean score is eight
point four, its pressure reproduction error is zero point three
percent, and its vapor-composition error is zero point two percentage
point.

40-07
00:50:45.468 --> 00:50:48.710
Apply each stated teaching threshold separately.

40-08
00:50:48.890 --> 00:50:55.888
Explain how apparently favorable area and modeling results can coexist
with the local result.

40-09
00:50:56.068 --> 00:51:01.892
Pause for about twelve minutes and finish H one through H four without
the solutions.

41-01
00:51:04.422 --> 00:51:13.419
For H one, the center derivative is zero point four eight minus zero
point three two, divided by zero point two.

41-02
00:51:13.599 --> 00:51:15.498
That is zero point eight.

41-03
00:51:15.678 --> 00:51:24.958
Subtract the supplied log ratio, zero point seven five, to obtain a
local residual of zero point zero five.

41-04
00:51:25.138 --> 00:51:28.039
This is one dimensionless residual.

41-05
00:51:28.219 --> 00:51:36.542
It is not the full-dataset mean score, and the question does not
provide enough interior points to reconstruct that score.

41-06
00:51:36.722 --> 00:51:41.800
For H two, the correct status is not assessed for this local verdict.

41-07
00:51:41.980 --> 00:51:50.102
The path is isobaric and temperature varies, but the necessary
independent excess-enthalpy correction is missing.

41-08
00:51:50.282 --> 00:51:55.808
A raw value above the numerical threshold does not repair that missing
assumption.

41-09
00:51:55.988 --> 00:52:06.078
For H four, the area criterion is met, the local criterion is outside
its threshold, and both model-reproduction criteria are met.

41-10
00:52:06.258 --> 00:52:08.541
These statements can all be true.

41-11
00:52:08.721 --> 00:52:14.609
A global integral and a selected-model fit do not erase a local
departure.

41-12
00:52:14.789 --> 00:52:22.234
Investigate the data path, property assumptions and numerical
sensitivity before deciding its cause.

42-01
00:52:24.764 --> 00:52:32.615
For H three, subtract zero point one five from zero point three nine,
giving zero point two four.

42-02
00:52:32.795 --> 00:52:37.147
Divide by the endpoint distance, zero point six zero.

42-03
00:52:37.327 --> 00:52:44.175
The beta fraction is zero point four zero, and the alpha fraction is
zero point six zero.

42-04
00:52:44.355 --> 00:52:56.024
The balance is zero point six zero times zero point one five plus zero
point four zero times zero point seven five, giving zero point three
nine.

42-05
00:52:56.204 --> 00:53:01.346
That verifies that these amounts and compositions are compatible with
the feed.

42-06
00:53:01.526 --> 00:53:04.491
It does not yet establish equilibrium.

42-07
00:53:04.671 --> 00:53:15.429
The candidate phases must be distinct and have equal component
chemical potentials, and their common tangent must support the full
admitted Gibbs curve.

42-08
00:53:15.609 --> 00:53:24.889
Because that curve was not supplied in this question, the correct
conclusion identifies the missing global evidence rather than
inventing it.

42-09
00:53:25.069 --> 00:53:30.765
We finish by submitting a short reflection supported by the evidence
you generated.

43-01
00:53:33.295 --> 00:53:40.186
Open the existing Session Three exit-ticket and feedback form using
the link or QR code.

43-02
00:53:40.366 --> 00:53:43.459
Follow section I on worksheet page six.

43-03
00:53:43.639 --> 00:53:50.420
In the concept answer, explain when a calculated liquid split
represents stable equilibrium.

43-04
00:53:50.600 --> 00:53:59.389
Include the distinction between a metastable homogeneous feed and the
